Impressively thorough test suite. Let's run it, then run the actual CLI against snapshot-a then snapshot-b to verify real behavior.
Need Python 3.11+. Let's find it.
All tests pass. Now let's manually run the actual CLI against the fixtures to verify real-world behavior, in a scratch copy.
Good, no stray sqlite3 file in the actual run-1 dir (tests use tempdir). The one in scratch must've been created by something else — let's check.
First run sends 6 items × 2 matching channels + firehose. Now run again on snapshot-a (should dedupe) and then snapshot-b (should get only genuinely new items).
Dedup works on repeated runs. Now switch to snapshot-b.
No changes to feeds.py, channels.py, render.py, or config — minimal footprint. Let's look at the full digest.py and store.py diffs more carefully.
Let's independently verify the partial-failure semantics with a manual script (not relying on the agent's own tests), and double check the locking doesn't deadlock or misbehave.
Confirmed independently: `ops` (which succeeded first) is not resent; `energy` (which failed) and `everything` (never attempted) both retry correctly on the next run. Now let's check for dead code, vestigial columns, and any issues with the `raw_id` column now that identity no longer uses it.
Good, clean. Now let's check the `tests/` directory's relationship to the repo (new dependency added, any scope creep) and confirm `--dry-run` truly doesn't touch the ledger/lock by re-checking manually, plus check what happens on a genuinely fresh DB from scratch (first run).
Matches the documented behavior exactly. Now let's check the `config.example.toml` for webhook channel type to confirm nothing there conflicts, and double check `channels.py` behavior is untouched (still raises on failure, propagating — matches digest.py's new locking).
Let me verify feed-failure handling (a feed erroring shouldn't lose or duplicate its pending items) with an independent manual test.
Let's test the actual webhook delivery path (not covered by the agent's own test suite, which only exercises `stdout` channels) to check for integration bugs.
Real webhook delivery works correctly and dedupes. No bugs found in that path either. Let's check one more potential edge case: concurrent real OS processes (not threads) hitting the same DB, to validate the locking claims hold up outside of the agent's own threaded test.
I reviewed the agent's diff against the pristine codebase, read every changed file in full, and independently exercised the solution beyond its own test suite:

- Ran both fixture snapshots through the real CLI and confirmed the three deliberately-planted trap cases (rotating `utm_campaign`, missing `blogroll` ID, regenerated `wire` guid) all dedupe correctly, with only the genuinely new item sent on snapshot-b.
- Independently simulated a mid-run channel failure, a feed outage, a pre-existing-database upgrade, a real webhook receiver, and two real concurrent OS processes sharing one SQLite file — all behaved as documented.
- Diffed every file against the original to confirm `feeds.py`/`channels.py`/`render.py`/`config.example.toml` are byte-identical and `--dry-run` still works.

Findings: the agent correctly identified that permalink (not `raw_id`/`guid`) is the only identifier stable across all three feeds, built a per-channel `deliveries` ledger instead of relying on the unconditional `items` archive, added `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` locking for cron overlap, and wrote a README section that explicitly names and resolves all three ambiguity forks (per-channel suppression, edited-item-as-same, no-backfill-on-upgrade) plus argues the at-least-once tradeoff. I found no bugs. The only real gaps are operational rather than correctness ones: no retention/pruning story for the ever-growing archive/ledger tables, and no scripted way to reset dedup for a single channel short of deleting the whole database.

Score: 40/40 (10/8/8/6/4/4), would merge as-is. Full writeup with code-quoted evidence per category is in `_review.md`.
